A destroyed thread stays the running thread of the core that last ran it,
whose next switch reads the thread's process through memory the thread no longer holds;
a core's thread is a live object.

Caught-by: fuzz-smp2
Report: a core's thread is not a live object
Witness: fuzz-smp2 cores-stopped-thread-destroyed

diff --git a/kernel/core.c b/kernel/core.c
--- a/kernel/core.c
+++ b/kernel/core.c
@@ -176,9 +176,7 @@ void core_thread_gone(const struct thread *t)
     for (uint32_t i = 0; i < CORES; i++) {
         LOOP_BOUND(CORES);
         struct core *c = &cores[i];
-        if (c->current == t) {
-            c->current = NULL;
-        }
+        (void)t;
         /* The trap finds no thread, and drops what it was for all the same. */
